{"results":[{"ok":true,"spec":{"query":"Power BI gateway monitoring commercial SaaS product 2024 2025","limit":6},"result":[{"url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/data-integration/gateway/service-gateway-performance","title":"Monitor and optimize on-premises data gateway performance","domain":"learn.microsoft.com","summary":"## Gateway performance monitoring (public preview)\nTo monitor performance, gateway admins traditionally depended on manually monitoring performance counters through the Windows Performance Monitor tool.\nWe now offer extra query logging and a Gateway Performance PBI template file to visualize the results.\nThis feature provides new insights into gateway usage.\nYou can use it to troubleshoot slow-performing queries.\nNote\nThis feature is currently available only for the on-premises data gateway in the standard mode.\nIt's not available for the personal mode.\n...\nGateway diagnostics doesn't capture diagnostics directly related to the (virtual) machine and its network, like bandwidth or latency.\nHowever, these diagnostics might affect your gateway performance.\nYou can use resource monitoring tools to monitor your machine.\n...\nThis feature is now turned on by default.\n...\n- Currently, queries from premium capacity to the gateway are sometimes missed in this logging.\nWe're actively working on fixing this issue.\n- Currently, Power BI paginated report queries aren't logged using this tool.\n...\nThere are other values in the config file `C:\\Program Files\\On-premises data gateway\\Microsoft.PowerBI.DataMovement.Pipeline.GatewayCore.dll.config` that you can update as needed:\n- **ReportFilePath**: Determines the path where the four log files are stored.\nBy default, this path is either *\\Users\\PBIEgwService\\AppData\\Local\\Microsoft\\On-premises data gateway\\Report* or *\\Windows\\ServiceProfiles\\PBIEgwService\\AppData\\Local\\Microsoft\\On-premises data gateway\\Report*.\nThe path depends on the OS version.\nIf you use a service account for the gateway other than *PBIEgwService*, replace this part of the path with the service account name.\n- **ReportFileCount**: Determines the number of log files of each kind to retain.\nThe default value is 10.\n- **ReportFileSizeInBytes**: Determines the size of the file to maintain.\nThe default value is 104,857,600.\n- **QueryExecutionAggregationTimeInMinutes**: Determines the number of minutes for which the query execution information is aggregated.\nThe default value is 5.\n- **SystemCounterAggregationTimeInMinutes**: Determines the number of minutes for which the system counter is aggregated.\nThe default value is 5.\nAfter you make the changes to the config file, restart the gateway for these config values to take effect.\nThe report files are now being generated in the location that you specified for **ReportFilePath**.\n...\nWhen you turn on this feature, four new log files are created:\n- The Query Execution Report\n- The Query Start Report\n- The Query Execution Aggregation Report\n- The System Counter Aggregation Report\n...\nNow, you can visualize the data that's in the log files.\n1. Download the Gateway Performance PBI template, and open it using Power BI Desktop.\n2. In the dialog that opens, check that the folder path matches the value in **ReportFilePath**.\n3. Select **Load**, and the template file starts loading the data from your log files.\nAll visuals are populated by using the data in the reports.\n4. Optionally, save this file as a PBIX, and publish it to your service for automatic refreshes.\nTo learn more, go to Publish semantic models and reports from Power BI Desktop.\n...\nIf you're investigating slow-performing queries, in addition to using the performance monitoring feature, you can temporarily enable **Additional logging** to gather more log information, including mashup engine logs, query strings, and verbose-level tracing.\nThese logs are written to the same place as your normal gateway logs.\nTo enable extra logging, in the on-premises data gateway app select **Diagnostics** > **Additional logging**.\n...\n1. In the C:\\Program Files\\On-Premises data gateway\\Microsoft.PowerBI.DataMovement.Pipeline.GatewayCore.dll.config file, set the **StreamBeforeRequestCompletes** setting to **True**, and then save.\n```\n<setting name=\"StreamBeforeRequestCompletes\" serializeAs=\"String\">\n<value>True</value>\n</setting>\n```\n2. In **On-premises data gateway** > **Service Settings**, restart the gateway.","date":"2025-06-12"},{"url":"https://www.summitview.app/features/gateway-monitoring","title":"Power BI Gateway Monitoring | SummitView","domain":"www.summitview.app","summary":"# Spot personal gateways and connectivity risks before they cause failures.\n- Find every personal gateway hiding in your tenant\n- Know the instant a gateway goes offline\n- See which datasets break when a gateway fails\n14-day free trial · No credit card · 5-minute setup\nGateway Monitoring\n...\nWith SummitView\n- Every gateway and connection cataloged, including personal ones\n- Online/offline monitoring with version tracking\n- Dependency mapping shows which datasets are at risk\n...\nIdentify unmanaged gateways tied to individual machines and users.\n...\nTrack online/offline status and alert when gateways go down.\n...\nTenant-wide gateway inventory\nenterprise and personal\nOnline/offline status monitoring with alerting\nPersonal gateway connection flagging with governance risk indicators\nData source count and type tracking per gateway\nVersion tracking with outdated-version warnings\nDependency mapping\nsee which datasets depend on which gateways\nAffected-dataset context in every alert\nknow the blast radius instantly\n...\n1\nConnect via Cloud Connect or the optional agent — SummitView enumerates all gateways and data sources automatically.\n2\nGateway health, version, and type are monitored continuously.\n3\nPersonal gateways and offline gateways trigger alerts with affected-dataset context.\n...\nNo matter your Power BI license, gateway monitoring is included.\nPower BI Pro\nCore monitoring, usage tracking, alerting\nPremium Per User\nAll Pro features + per-table timing, paginated reports\nMicrosoft Fabric\nAll PPU features + capacity metrics, throttling detection\nGateway Monitoring works with all Power BI license types.\nIt monitors gateways at the tenant level — both enterprise gateways managed by IT and personal gateways installed by individual users.\n...\nStart your 14-day free trial.\nNo credit card required.\nSet up in 5 minutes.\n$299/mo per tenant\nUnlimited users\nAll features included","date":"2026-02-01"},{"url":"https://marketplace.microsoft.com/en-us/product/saas/carloconsultingllc1611096483318.fabric_monitor_02?tab=overview","title":"Business Intelligence Monitoring Accelerator and Support","domain":"marketplace.microsoft.com","summary":"# Business Intelligence Monitoring Accelerator and Support\n## by Carlo Consulting LLC\n## Full End to End Power BI and Microsoft Fabric Monitoring Deployment Solution and Support\nIntroducing a comprehensive Data Monitoring solution designed specifically for technical leaders who manage Power BI and Microsoft Fabric environments.\nThis accelerator is engineered to pull data from Microsoft Power BI APIs, Microsoft Fabric APIs, and Microsoft Graph APIs, creating a holistic view of activities and a detailed catalog of items across your entire Power BI and Microsoft Fabric Tenant.\n...\nGateway Monitoring: Monitor the health and performance of your data gateways to ensure seamless data flow and connectivity.\n...\nWe also provide technical support for the Fabric Unified Admin Monitoring (FUAM) project, an open-source initiative that provides extensive monitoring capabilities for Power BI and Fabric environments.","date":"2025-11-08"},{"url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0Enini93Quk","title":"How To Monitor Your On-prem Power BI Gateways - YouTube","domain":"www.youtube.com","summary":"In this Power BI Dev Camp session, Rui demonstrates the pbigtwmonitor solution which he has published to GitHub.\nBy learning how to test and deploy this solution, you can learn how to help Power BI admins quickly access all gateway logs for an entire Power BI tenant and to explore the gateway logs, counters and queries on a pre-built Power BI Report.\n...\nIn the complete way, because you can you t use the power BI template.\nI will show you how to do\n{ts:389} it, but if you want to deploy this and if you have a lot t of gateways the main advantage that this solution will l give you it will centralize it\n{ts:399} it will make sure that all the gateway logs they will be.\nUploaded and centralized in the data link and existing data link or the new data link if you want.\n{ts:409} And that, and the way I do it is it's basically having a Powershell script that is deployed on each gateway server.\nSo you need to deploy this script on each gateway server and\n{ts:420} d that script is very simple and lightweight so you can have it running g.\nI won't say every minute, but every 15 minutes every y 30 minutes and the script will save\n{ts:431} e the last execution time and every time it runs it t will get the new logs since the last time it's run n and upload that new log.\n{ts:439} To the Gateway server and the other thing that the script will do is it will apply y some metadata the to the file so it\n{ts:449} t will discover the gateway ID, it will classify each log by year, month and day and partition that in separate folders and it also classified the type of log\n{ts:459} g that are in the gateway logs you have just t verbals and error logs but you also get some report t logs that will get that will log information like.\n{ts:469} Every query that is running on the gateway and also some metadata logs that are very important for example logging.\nHow\n...\nThe BLOB storage or the\n{ts:490} data lake will have a folder for each gateway and on each folder you have the logs.\n...\nThe most important things are the\n{ts:749} e scripts.\nSo this is the script that you need to deploy and run on each on each gateway server it\n{ts:756} t has a configuration file.\nThat configuration file the only thing that you should.\nBe required to change is the\n{ts:764} connection string the to the storage account or to the e data lake, everything else.\n...\nYou can do, you just need to download the front desk if you want to the lake and\n{ts:840} have a complete automation of the whole thing.\nYou need to use the from lake, but it's exactly 1 ne more time exactly the same template, so","date":"2023-02-16"},{"url":"https://3cloudsolutions.com/resources/monitoring-power-bi-on-premises-data-gateway-performance/","title":"Monitoring Power BI On-Premises Data Gateway Performance","domain":"3cloudsolutions.com","summary":"In June 2019, Microsoft made Gateway Monitoring much easier via the addition of structured logs that can be enabled in the configuration file, and by providing a starter Gateway Performance Power BI report template to visualize the results.\n...\nAs of January 2020, there are four log files, and these are the main things you need to know to follow along:\n...\nHowever, in the long run, we recommend implementing a full-fledged solution to extract and store these files in a designated Power BI Monitoring Database, coupled  with additional Usage Auditing and Tenant Inventory data.\n...\nIn June 2019, Microsoft released a report template, which is a great place to start to get data loaded and processed.\n...\n**The Gateway Performance Report template,** developed by BlueGranite, uses Microsoft’s as a starting point but expands and builds upon it to provide an actionable, easy-to-navigate, and use, report.\n...\nInstructions on how to set up Gateway Logging and the Microsoft Gateway Performance Power BI template download are available here.","date":"2020-02-11"},{"url":"https://blog.crossjoin.co.uk/2024/10/27/new-solution-for-monitoring-power-bi-fabric-gateways/","title":"New Solution For Monitoring Power BI/Fabric Gateways","domain":"blog.crossjoin.co.uk","summary":"A while ago Rui Romano published a gateway monitoring solution that proved to be very popular, but recently my colleague at Microsoft Edgar Cotte built a new open-source solution that builds on Rui’s work and which is even more powerful, using Fabric Real-Time Intelligence to let you analyse gateway log information in real time.\nYou can read his announcement on LinkedIn here and download the solution from https://aka.ms/FabricGatewayMonitoring.\nThis is an amazing piece of work that is already being used by several very large customers.","date":"2024-10-27"}],"error":null},{"ok":true,"spec":{"query":"pbi monitor gateway enterprise product observability","limit":6},"result":[{"url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/data-integration/gateway/service-gateway-performance","title":"Monitor and optimize on-premises data gateway performance","domain":"learn.microsoft.com","summary":"To monitor performance, gateway admins traditionally depended on manually monitoring performance counters through the Windows Performance Monitor tool.\nWe now offer extra query logging and a Gateway Performance PBI template file to visualize the results.\nThis feature provides new insights into gateway usage.\nYou can use it to troubleshoot slow-performing queries.\nNote\nThis feature is currently available only for the on-premises data gateway in the standard mode.\nIt's not available for the personal mode.\n...\nGateway diagnostics doesn't capture diagnostics directly related to the (virtual) machine and its network, like bandwidth or latency.\nHowever, these diagnostics might affect your gateway performance.\nYou can use resource monitoring tools to monitor your machine.\n...\nThis feature is now turned on by default.\n...\nBy default, this path is either *\\Users\\PBIEgwService\\AppData\\Local\\Microsoft\\On-premises data gateway\\Report* or *\\Windows\\ServiceProfiles\\PBIEgwService\\AppData\\Local\\Microsoft\\On-premises data gateway\\Report*.\nThe path depends on the OS version.\n...\nAfter you make the changes to the config file, restart the gateway for these config values to take effect.\nThe report files are now being generated in the location that you specified for **ReportFilePath**.\n...\nWhen you turn on this feature, four new log files are created:\n- The Query Execution Report\n- The Query Start Report\n- The Query Execution Aggregation Report\n- The System Counter Aggregation Report\n...\nNow, you can visualize the data that's in the log files.\n1. Download the Gateway Performance PBI template, and open it using Power BI Desktop.\n2. In the dialog that opens, check that the folder path matches the value in **ReportFilePath**.\n3. Select **Load**, and the template file starts loading the data from your log files.\nAll visuals are populated by using the data in the reports.\n4. Optionally, save this file as a PBIX, and publish it to your service for automatic refreshes.\nTo learn more, go to Publish semantic models and reports from Power BI Desktop.\n...\nThese logs are written to the same place as your normal gateway logs.\nTo enable extra logging, in the on-premises data gateway app select **Diagnostics** > **Additional logging**.\n...\n1. In the C:\\Program Files\\On-Premises data gateway\\Microsoft.PowerBI.DataMovement.Pipeline.GatewayCore.dll.config file, set the **StreamBeforeRequestCompletes** setting to **True**, and then save.\n```\n<setting name=\"StreamBeforeRequestCompletes\" serializeAs=\"String\">\n<value>True</value>\n</setting>\n```\n2. In **On-premises data gateway** > **Service Settings**, restart the gateway.","date":"2025-06-12"},{"url":"https://www.summitview.app/features/gateway-monitoring","title":"Power BI Gateway Monitoring | SummitView","domain":"www.summitview.app","summary":"# Spot personal gateways and connectivity risks before they cause failures.\n- Find every personal gateway hiding in your tenant\n- Know the instant a gateway goes offline\n- See which datasets break when a gateway fails\n...\nWith SummitView\n- Every gateway and connection cataloged, including personal ones\n- Online/offline monitoring with version tracking\n- Dependency mapping shows which datasets are at risk\n...\n#### Personal Gateway Flagging\nIdentify unmanaged gateways tied to individual machines and users.\n#### Status Monitoring\nTrack online/offline status and alert when gateways go down.\n#### Version Tracking\nFlag outdated gateway versions that pose compatibility and security risks.\n#### Dependency Mapping\nSee which datasets depend on which gateways for impact analysis.\n...\nTenant-wide gateway inventory\nenterprise and personal\nOnline/offline status monitoring with alerting\nPersonal gateway connection flagging with governance risk indicators\nData source count and type tracking per gateway\nVersion tracking with outdated-version warnings\nDependency mapping\nsee which datasets depend on which gateways\nAffected-dataset context in every alert\nknow the blast radius instantly\n...\nThree steps.\nNo code changes.\nNo Power BI modifications.\n1\nConnect via Cloud Connect or the optional agent — SummitView enumerates all gateways and data sources automatically.\n2\nGateway health, version, and type are monitored continuously.\n3\nPersonal gateways and offline gateways trigger alerts with affected-dataset context.\n...\nNo matter your Power BI license, gateway monitoring is included.\nPower BI Pro\nCore monitoring, usage tracking, alerting\nPremium Per User\n...\nGateway Monitoring works with all Power BI license types.\nIt monitors gateways at the tenant level — both enterprise gateways managed by IT and personal gateways installed by individual users.\n...\nStart your 14-day free trial.\nNo credit card required.\nSet up in 5 minutes.","date":"2026-02-01"},{"url":"https://www.minceddata.info/2023/10/16/power-bi-gateway-monitoring-and-more-using-microsoft-fabric/","title":"Power BI Gateway monitoring and more using Microsoft Fabric","domain":"www.minceddata.info","summary":"For this reason, I consider it a good idea to track the well-being of these valuable resources.\nThis article describes a solution built with Microsoft Fabric.\nIt’s not necessary to use Fabric, and it’s also not necessary to build a solution on your own.\nIf you want to track the well-being of your on-premises data gateways but do not want to build something, I recommend using the solution by Rui Romano you can find here: https://github.com/RuiRomano/pbigtwmonitor\nI built this monitoring solution focusing on the well-being of the on-premises data gateway.\nI might extend this solution in the future, but for now, it’s about the availability of the on-premises data gateway and the data gateway connections.\n...\nI use Microsoft Fabric because I do not have to care about getting access to many different Azure resources.\nI only need a Microsoft Fabric-enabled workspace and a Service Principal.\nWhile the components of Microsoft Fabric are automatically available when using a Microsoft Fabric-enabled workspace, I have to take care of the Service Principal.\nHaving easy access to notebooks (for Python coding), pipelines (for orchestration and automation), a lakehouse (for data storage), and, of course, Power BI makes the promise of an integrated SaaS data analytics platform come true.\n...\nThese resources are the Power BI REST APIs and the Service Principal.\n...\nFor tracking the well-being of the on-premises data gateway(s), I use the REST API endpoint\n`gateways` described here: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/rest/api/power-bi/gateways/get-gateways.\nLater, I will extend this solution to\nother REST APIs from the gateway group.\nI consider monitoring the Power BI environment a good idea, so I consider using the Power BI REST APIs essential.\nBut the REST APIs are not only useful when you want to monitor your Power BI tenant.\nThey also help in automating tasks like workspace creation and many more\n...\nYou can read about the creation of a Service Principal in this article: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/developer/embedded/embed-service-principal?WT.mc_id=DP-MVP-5003068.\nFrom my experience, it’s always a good idea to add the Service Principal to an Azure Active Directory (Azure Entra ID) security group, the reason for this is simple, sometimes (maybe most of the time) it’s not possible to provide permissions to the Service Principal directly inside Power BI.\nInstead, permissions can only be granted via an Azure Entra ID security group.\n...\nFor this reason, you can allow specific REST APIs to be executed by a service principal.\nThis assignment is done in the API permissions blade of the service principal, this is shown in the following visualization:\nThe following image shows how you allow the execution of Power BI REST APIs by the security group the Service Principal is assigned to:\nUsing security groups instead of adding the service principal directly is always a good idea.\nGranting permissions to a service principal should always follow the concept of least privileges.\nFollowing this concept leads to the creation of multiple service principals.\nThis means you need documentation of the service principals you allow access to the APIs of your service - the Power BI Service.\nHowever, documentation of the various service principals is a different topic.\nThe following image shows how you add the Service Principal (not the security group, this is only for demonstration purposes) as an Admin to the on-premises data gateway(s) you want to monitor, the second resource that requires authorization next to the REST API.\n...\nHere, you will find the notebook “tenantMonitoring_gateways.ipynb” (https://tommartens-my.sharepoint.com/:u:/g/personal/tom_minceddata_com/EaxaEdtqMo5FvUdSstAAuoEBmo2PUYj4kXdqEqLCjoFUhA?e=MrF5eU).\n...\nBasically, the current solution is simple: I have a Fabric-enabled workspace, a lakehouse, and a notebook, but the solution will grow during the next iterations.\n...\nInside the lakehouse, I created a subfolder “GatewayInformation,” inside the files section.\nThis is the place where I store the result of a REST API endpoint request as a CSV file, of course, after some data transformations.\nI write the same dataframe (almost the same) that I have written as CSV also as a table in delta format using the vorder sorting.","date":"2023-10-16"},{"url":"https://3cloudsolutions.com/resources/monitoring-power-bi-on-premises-data-gateway-performance/","title":"Monitoring Power BI On-Premises Data Gateway Performance","domain":"3cloudsolutions.com","summary":"Gateway Performance Monitoring is a frequently overlooked but important part of Power BI governance.\nMany people think that as long as all data refreshes are successful through the gateway, and the gateway status is online, there is no reason to bother setting up a full-fledged monitoring and reporting process.\n...\nIn this blog post we describe how to gather necessary gateway log data, and providing a detailed overview of the report we designed to leverage it.\n...\nIn June 2019, Microsoft made Gateway Monitoring much easier via the addition of structured logs that can be enabled in the configuration file, and by providing a starter Gateway Performance Power BI report template to visualize the results.\nThese logs give full access to the information, minimizing the need to pull counter data from IT management systems, like the Microsoft System Center Operations Manager (SCOM); or run the Windows Performance Toolkit and dig through dust-covered gateway info and error log files; or pull refresh error messages from the Power BI Service.\nEnabling structured Gateway Monitoring logging doesn’t add any significant load to the server, so that’s another win.\nAs of January 2020, there are four log files, and these are the main things you need to know to follow along:\n- **Query Execution **– Information for each individual query that was issued to an on-premises data source.\nIt also has data about a parent Request ID.\nFor example, user interacting with a visual in DirectQuery mode or user running on-demand dataset refresh each issue a series of various queries with two distinct Request IDs.\n- **Query Start** – This one is essentially an extension of the Query Execution log with information about Query Start Time in Coordinated Universal Time (UTC) and base64 encoded full Query Text.\nIn the context of the questions we are aiming to answer, this is pure gold!\n- **Query Execution Aggregation** – Query Execution data aggregated for a predefined period grouped by data source, query type (DQ or Import Mode Refresh) and Query Status (success or a failure).\n- **System Counter Aggregation** – Base performance data on CPU and Memory counters for a predefined aggregation period, grouped by counter.\nIt captures data about system, gateway, and mashup engine.\n...\nThere are a few additional parameters related to these logs that can be controlled in a main configuration file, such as aggregation period span, log file size, and number of log files of the same type to keep.\nIt makes sense to change the last two from defaults when a centralized extraction and reporting process is established.\n...\nFor organizations with less complex or voluminous data, a process to copy all log files from each respective gateway machine to a central location (file system or a data lake) may be preferred over the more robust architecture defined below.\nHowever, in the long run, we recommend implementing a full-fledged solution to extract and store these files in a designated Power BI Monitoring Database, coupled  with additional Usage Auditing and Tenant Inventory data.\n...\nIn June 2019, Microsoft released a report template, which is a great place to start to get data loaded and processed.\n...\n**The Gateway Performance Report template,** developed by BlueGranite, uses Microsoft’s as a starting point but expands and builds upon it to provide an actionable, easy-to-navigate, and use, report.\n...\nIt is worth a reminder that the picture will not be complete without also establishing the Usage Auditing and Tenant Inventory reporting mentioned earlier, and described in our companion blog post.\n...\nInstructions on how to set up Gateway Logging and the Microsoft Gateway Performance Power BI template download are available here.","date":"2020-02-11"},{"url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vGbfDScNU6A","title":"PBIPT #43 - Power BI Gateway Monitoring","domain":"www.youtube.com","summary":"challenge faced by Power BI Admins is how-to monitor & troubleshoot multiple on-premises Data Gateway Clusters.\nWith each cluster having multiple servers that are responsible for connecting their data sources to the Power BI service in the cloud, investigating errors or performance issues across the gateway logs can become a complex task.\nThis presentation aims to show a solution to help Power BI admins to:\n• Quickly access all gateway logs\n• Explore the gateway logs, counters & queries on a pre-built Power BI Report\nPower BI Gateway Monitor solution:\nhttps://github.com/RuiRomano/pbigtwmonitor\n…","date":"2022-09-28"},{"url":"https://blog.crossjoin.co.uk/2024/10/27/new-solution-for-monitoring-power-bi-fabric-gateways/","title":"New Solution For Monitoring Power BI/Fabric Gateways","domain":"blog.crossjoin.co.uk","summary":"A while ago Rui Romano published a gateway monitoring solution that proved to be very popular, but recently my colleague at Microsoft Edgar Cotte built a new open-source solution that builds on Rui’s work and which is even more powerful, using Fabric Real-Time Intelligence to let you analyse gateway log information in real time.\nYou can read his announcement on LinkedIn here and download the solution from https://aka.ms/FabricGatewayMonitoring.","date":"2024-10-27"}],"error":null},{"ok":true,"spec":{"query":"BI Elite Fabric monitoring gateway performance tool","limit":6},"result":[{"url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/data-integration/gateway/service-gateway-performance","title":"Monitor and optimize on-premises data gateway performance","domain":"learn.microsoft.com","summary":"To monitor performance, gateway admins traditionally depended on manually monitoring performance counters through the Windows Performance Monitor tool.\nWe now offer extra query logging and a Gateway Performance PBI template file to visualize the results.\nThis feature provides new insights into gateway usage.\nYou can use it to troubleshoot slow-performing queries.\nNote\nThis feature is currently available only for the on-premises data gateway in the standard mode.\nIt's not available for the personal mode.\n...\nThis feature is now turned on by default.\n...\nAfter you make the changes to the config file, restart the gateway for these config values to take effect.\nThe report files are now being generated in the location that you specified for **ReportFilePath**.\n...\nWhen you turn on this feature, four new log files are created:\n- The Query Execution Report\n- The Query Start Report\n- The Query Execution Aggregation Report\n- The System Counter Aggregation Report\n...\nNow, you can visualize the data that's in the log files.\n1. Download the Gateway Performance PBI template, and open it using Power BI Desktop.\n2. In the dialog that opens, check that the folder path matches the value in **ReportFilePath**.\n3. Select **Load**, and the template file starts loading the data from your log files.\nAll visuals are populated by using the data in the reports.\n4. Optionally, save this file as a PBIX, and publish it to your service for automatic refreshes.\nTo learn more, go to Publish semantic models and reports from Power BI Desktop.\n...\nTo enable extra logging, in the on-premises data gateway app select **Diagnostics** > **Additional logging**.\n...\n1. In the C:\\Program Files\\On-Premises data gateway\\Microsoft.PowerBI.DataMovement.Pipeline.GatewayCore.dll.config file, set the **StreamBeforeRequestCompletes** setting to **True**, and then save.\n```\n<setting name=\"StreamBeforeRequestCompletes\" serializeAs=\"String\">\n<value>True</value>\n</setting>\n```\n2. In **On-premises data gateway** > **Service Settings**, restart the gateway.","date":"2025-06-12"},{"url":"https://community.fabric.microsoft.com/t5/Fabric-platform/How-to-Monitor-Usage-Across-Multiple-Virtual-Network-Data/m-p/4864674","title":"Re: How to Monitor Usage Across Multiple Virtual N...","domain":"community.fabric.microsoft.com","summary":"You can see the VNet Gateway CUs in the Fabric Capacity Metrics App.\nDon't forget!!!\n...\nAt the moment, **Microsoft Fabric does not expose CU (Capacity Unit) consumption at the individual data gateway level**, whether for on-premises or virtual network data gateways.\nHowever, here’s what you *can* do:\n1) Install the Fabric Capacity Metrics App to monitor CU by **workspace and workload type**\nDocs :\n- https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/enterprise/metrics-app\n- https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/enterprise/metrics-app-install?tabs=1st\n2) **Gateway activity – Audit and diagnostic logs**\nWhile CU-level data isn’t tracked, you can **monitor connection and query activity** per gateway via:\n- **Microsoft Fabric Audit Logs** (Power BI Admin → Audit log search)\n- **Gateway Diagnostic Logs** from the Power Platform Admin Center or Azure Log Analytics\nThese include:\n- Number of queries and connections\n- Average duration\n- Failed vs successful requests\nThis lets you indirectly identify if a gateway is overused or idle.\nDocs :\n- https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/data-integration/gateway/service-gateway-performance\nHope it can help you ! ","date":"2025-11-07"},{"url":"https://jumpstart.fabric.microsoft.com/catalog/fpm-gateway-monitoring/","title":"Fabric Platform Monitoring - Gateway Monitoring - Fabric Jumpstart","domain":"jumpstart.fabric.microsoft.com","summary":"This jumpstart deploys an end-to-end **On-Premises Data Gateway observability stack** into your Microsoft Fabric workspace — two Eventstreams that receive heartbeat and report telemetry from a PowerShell uploader running on the gateway host, an Eventhouse-backed\n```\nGateway Monitoring\n```\nKQL database that durably stores every event, and a DirectQuery Power BI semantic model + report with six built-in pages (Jobs, Job Details, Queries, Running Jobs, Gateways, System Counters) for live operational insight.\n⏱️ **Deploy time: ~2 minutes.** Plan another ~5 minutes inside Fabric for the walkthrough — plus a separate one-time install of the PowerShell uploader on each On-Premises Data Gateway machine you want to monitor.\n...\n|``` Gateway Monitoring ```|SemanticModel|DirectQuery Power BI model over the KQL database.\nSix query-group parameters —\n``` KustoURL ``` ,\n``` KustoDB ``` ,\n``` DaysBack ``` ,\n``` DateToSearch ``` ,\n``` SelectedGateways ``` ,\n``` SelectedClusters ``` — drive four DirectQuery tables (\n``` Gateway Node Info ``` ,\n``` Query Execution ``` ,\n``` Query Connections ``` ,\n``` System Counters ``` ) plus import tables for\n``` Calendar ``` ,\n``` Period ``` ,\n``` Execution Measures ``` , and the counter measure parameter.\n``` Execution Measures ``` contains 18 measures for jobs, queries, durations, and disk I/O.|\n|``` Gateway Monitoring ```|Report|Power BI report bound to the semantic model.\nSix pages: Jobs, Job Details (drillthrough), Queries, Running Jobs, Gateways, System Counters.\nPage refresh is enabled on Jobs, Queries, and System Counters at 5 minutes, Running Jobs at 10 seconds, and Gateways at 30 seconds.|\n...\nin the current workspace.\n...\n## Try It — A 5-Minute Walkthrough\n1. **Install**—\n```\njumpstart.install(\"fpm-gateway-monitoring\")\n```\nand wait for the ~2-minute deploy.\n2. **Generate the config**— open\n```\nGateway Monitoring Setup\n```\n, set\n```"},{"url":"https://community.powerbi.com/t5/Power-Query/Monitoring-On-Premises-Data-Gateway/td-p/56371","title":"Monitoring On-Premises Data Gateway - Microsoft Fabric Community","domain":"community.powerbi.com","summary":"I've been using the gateway and other performance monitor counters via PBI Desktop on a project recently.","date":"2019-06-07"},{"url":"https://community.fabric.microsoft.com/t5/Service/Monitoring-vnet-data-gateway-service-and-statistics/td-p/4053912","title":"Monitoring vnet data gateway service and statistics","domain":"community.fabric.microsoft.com","summary":"You can download logs directly from the Power BI service.\nGo to the Manage connections and gateways page, select the Virtual network data gateways tab, and then locate the VNet data gateway.\nYou can download diagnostic logs, which include system counters, mashup logs, and query execution reports.\nYou can check the following link:\nDownload logs on the VNet data gateway | Microsoft Learn\nYou can use PowerShell to retrieve performance statistics.\nFirst, use Connect to an Azure subscription.\nConnect-AzAccount can then retrieve the data gateway resource group and name to get the necessary performance data.\n...\nAzure Monitor can be used to collect and aggregate metrics and logs from your VNet data gateway.\nThis service provides a comprehensive view of availability, performance, and resiliency and can notify you of any issues.\nYou can refer to this link:\nMonitor Azure Virtual Network | Microsoft Learn\n...\nI also couldn't find any documentation in regards Monitoring the Vnet however I can suggest to try this template\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/data-integration/gateway/service-gateway-performance\nIf you can use the end point url of your Vnet and hopefully it has the same structure as the on pemises logs gateway.\nBut I believe you can be able to build your own report with Power BI desktop if you can connect to the Vnet end point.","date":"2025-02-03"},{"url":"https://blog.crossjoin.co.uk/2024/10/27/new-solution-for-monitoring-power-bi-fabric-gateways/","title":"New Solution For Monitoring Power BI/Fabric Gateways","domain":"blog.crossjoin.co.uk","summary":"A while ago Rui Romano published a gateway monitoring solution that proved to be very popular, but recently my colleague at Microsoft Edgar Cotte built a new open-source solution that builds on Rui’s work and which is even more powerful, using Fabric Real-Time Intelligence to let you analyse gateway log information in real time.\nYou can read his announcement on LinkedIn here and download the solution from https://aka.ms/FabricGatewayMonitoring.\n...\n1. This gets data from the gateway logs, the capacity metrics app shows data for CU usage","date":"2024-10-27"}],"error":null},{"ok":true,"spec":{"query":"Pragmatic Works Power BI gateway monitoring","limit":6},"result":[{"url":"https://pragmaticworks.com/courses/configurating-power-bi-data-gateways","title":"Configurating Power BI Data Gateways - Pragmatic Works","domain":"pragmaticworks.com","summary":"Install, configure, and manage Power BI Data Gateways with Justin Vogel.\nLearn Standard vs Personal modes, set up secure connections, use refresh or DirectQuery/Live, and keep them reliable with clusters and logs.\n...\nJustin walks through configuring data sources in the Power BI Service, mapping credentials, and choosing between scheduled refresh and DirectQuery/Live scenarios.\nYou’ll explore high availability with gateway clusters, tips for performance, and practical troubleshooting using logs and diagnostics."},{"url":"https://pragmaticworks.com/blog/power-bi-and-data-security-on-premises-data-gateway","title":"Power BI and Data Security - On-Premises Data Gateway","domain":"pragmaticworks.com","summary":"All data and information between the gateway and Power BI is encrypted.\n...\nThe gateway creates an outbound connection to the Azure Service Bus using a specific set of ports including TCP 443 which is used for Power BI (complete list of ports used).\n...\nThe data is then encrypted and compressed at the gateway and returned to Power BI.\nNo data is stored in the gateway and the data is encrypted for transit.","date":"2017-04-28"},{"url":"https://pragmaticworks.com/hubfs/Data%20Security%20in%20Power%20BI%20-%20April%202019.pdf","title":"PowerPoint Presentation","domain":"pragmaticworks.com","summary":"On-premises Data Gateway\nImage Source: Microsoft\n•\nAll communication and data \nin transit is encrypted with \nHTTPS\n•\nData Source credentials \nencrypted in service, key \nmaintained on-premises\n•\nOutgoing ports only, gateway \nmonitors service bus for \nrequests\n•\nNo data is stored on the \ngateway and the gateway \nservice account should not \nhave access to data sources\n•\nGateway access is limited on \nthe service\nThis is a Header"},{"url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0Enini93Quk","title":"How To Monitor Your On-prem Power BI Gateways - YouTube","domain":"www.youtube.com","summary":"In this Power BI Dev Camp session, Rui demonstrates the pbigtwmonitor solution which he has published to GitHub.\nBy learning how to test and deploy this solution, you can learn how to help Power BI admins quickly access all gateway logs for an entire Power BI tenant and to explore the gateway logs, counters and queries on a pre-built Power BI Report.\n...\n{ts:381} solution that.\nIn the complete way, because you can you t use the power BI template.\nI will show you how to do\n{ts:389} it, but if you want to deploy this and if you have a lot t of gateways the main advantage that this solution will l give you it will centralize it\n{ts:399} it will make sure that all the gateway logs they will be.\nUploaded and centralized in the data link and existing data link or the new data link if you want.\n{ts:409} And that, and the way I do it is it's basically having a Powershell script that is deployed on each gateway server.\nSo you need to deploy this script on each gateway server and\n{ts:420} d that script is very simple and lightweight so you can have it running g.\nI won't say every minute, but every 15 minutes every y 30 minutes and the script will save\n{ts:431} e the last execution time and every time it runs it t will get the new logs since the last time it's run n and upload that new log.\n{ts:439} To the Gateway server and the other thing that the script will do is it will apply y some metadata the to the file so it\n{ts:449} t will discover the gateway ID, it will classify each log by year, month and day and partition that in separate folders and it also classified the type of log\n{ts:459} g that are in the gateway logs you have just t verbals and error logs but you also get some report t logs that will get that will log information like.\n{ts:469} Every query that is running on the gateway and also some metadata logs that are very important for example logging.\n...\n{ts:529} easy access to the log, so you don't need as a power BI admin or a tenant t admin.\nYou don't need to give permissions to.\nTo the gateway servers remote desktop permissions\n{ts:539} to the Gateway service to get access to the logs and now you can just share a data Lake connection and an access to a folder or all the folders if you want.\n...\nThe most important things are the\n{ts:749} e scripts.\nSo this is the script that you need to deploy and run on each on each gateway server it\n{ts:756} t has a configuration file.\nThat configuration file the only thing that you should.\nBe required to change is the\n{ts:764} connection string the to the storage account or to the e data lake, everything else.\nYou probably can't leave it by default but if you change the location where the log files\n{ts:776} get stored you we also need to change the location, but by default if you don't do anything it's going to be stored in this location.\nAnd also.\n{ts:787} That's another thing that is very important is these two There are two part of the templates that you can use that is 1 called from disk and one from data\n…\nYou can do, you just need to download the front desk if you want to the lake and\n{ts:840} have a complete automation of the whole thing.\nYou need to use the from lake, but it's exactly 1 ne more time exactly the same template, so\n{ts:848} that is everything is documented and I'll can you set this up, I will.","date":"2023-02-16"},{"url":"https://www.linkedin.com/posts/wagnerchris_onprem-reporting-simplified-pbirs-as-the-activity-7355628113575206914-fjYw","title":"Christopher Wagner, MBA, MVP's Post - LinkedIn","domain":"www.linkedin.com","summary":"If yes, any connection you create (especially for an on-prem data source) on one Gateway must also be created in the other Gateways on the cluster, too.\n...\n🔐 Automate Power BI Admin Tasks with the Admin API\n▶️ Watch: https://prag.works/pbiapi Learn how to securely connect and manage your Power BI environment using service principals and API endpoints.\n⚙️ Register an Azure app and configure permissions 🧩 Enable tenant-level access and security group setup 📜 Run a PowerShell script to export all workspaces via the Admin API #PowerBI #PragmaticWorks #MicrosoftPartner","date":"2025-07-28"},{"url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vGbfDScNU6A","title":"PBIPT #43 - Power BI Gateway Monitoring","domain":"www.youtube.com","summary":"challenge faced by Power BI Admins is how-to monitor & troubleshoot multiple on-premises Data Gateway Clusters.\nWith each cluster having multiple servers that are responsible for connecting their data sources to the Power BI service in the cloud, investigating errors or performance issues across the gateway logs can become a complex task.\nThis presentation aims to show a solution to help Power BI admins to:\n• Quickly access all gateway logs\n• Explore the gateway logs, counters & queries on a pre-built Power BI Report\nPower BI Gateway Monitor solution:\nhttps://github.com/RuiRomano/pbigtwmonitor\n…","date":"2022-09-28"}],"error":null},{"ok":true,"spec":{"query":"Biccountant gateway monitoring Fabric Power BI blog artifact","limit":6},"result":[{"url":"https://www.minceddata.info/2023/10/16/power-bi-gateway-monitoring-and-more-using-microsoft-fabric/","title":"Power BI Gateway monitoring and more using Microsoft Fabric","domain":"www.minceddata.info","summary":"This article describes a solution built with Microsoft Fabric.\n...\nI built this monitoring solution focusing on the well-being of the on-premises data gateway.\n...\nI use Microsoft Fabric because I do not have to care about getting access to many different Azure resources.\nI only need a Microsoft Fabric-enabled workspace and a Service Principal.\nWhile the components of Microsoft Fabric are automatically available when using a Microsoft Fabric-enabled workspace, I have to take care of the Service Principal.\n...\nFor tracking the well-being of the on-premises data gateway(s), I use the REST API endpoint\n`gateways` described here: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/rest/api/power-bi/gateways/get-gateways.\nLater, I will extend this solution to\nother REST APIs from the gateway group.\n...\nThe following image shows how you add the Service Principal (not the security group, this is only for demonstration purposes) as an Admin to the on-premises data gateway(s) you want to monitor, the second resource that requires authorization next to the REST API.\n...\nHmm, this is where fun happens.\nThis is the first time that the artifact I publicly share is not a pbix file but Python code.\nHere, you will find the notebook “tenantMonitoring_gateways.ipynb” (https://tommartens-my.sharepoint.com/:u:/g/personal/tom_minceddata_com/EaxaEdtqMo5FvUdSstAAuoEBmo2PUYj4kXdqEqLCjoFUhA?e=MrF5eU).\nThe notebook contains inline documentation, and reflects also some of my Python learning.\n...\nBasically, the current solution is simple: I have a Fabric-enabled workspace, a lakehouse, and a notebook, but the solution will grow during the next iterations.\n...\nInside the lakehouse, I created a subfolder “GatewayInformation,” inside the files section.\nThis is the place where I store the result of a REST API endpoint request as a CSV file, of course, after some data transformations.\nI write the same dataframe (almost the same) that I have written as CSV also as a table in delta format using the vorder sorting.","date":"2023-10-16"},{"url":"https://community.fabric.microsoft.com/t5/Desktop/Fabric-Monitoring-Dashboard-in-Power-BI/td-p/4631116","title":"Solved: Fabric Monitoring Dashboard in Power BI","domain":"community.fabric.microsoft.com","summary":"In case if your requirement is just to monitor one capacity in your tenant then the default Fabric capacity metrics APP would suffice.\nIt is a direct query based semantic model so the data would be near real time.\n...\nThere isn't any out of the box solution available to monitor all the capacities in your tenant at one place.\nI can think of two options\n1. You can build a custom solution to run DAX queries against the Fabric capacity metrics semantic model and once the data is extracted you can them stream this data to a Fabric real time event stream in order to build real time dashboard to monitor all the capacities.\n...\n2. There a few external verndors who has built some solutions, you can pay them and get it configured.\nTiger Analytics Fabric Tenant Monitoring solution, other than capacity monitoring it includes audit logs, usage information, Fabric artifact catalog etc, They are planning to include the gateway monitoring capabilities as well.\nYou can reachout to me on Linkedin if you want to know further.\n...\nThere is  workspace monitoring which has a realtime dashboard template.\nIf you need the entire capacity you need to use the log analytics integration.\nNot sure if you can connect a realtime fabric dashboard to this, but you can definitely use direct query in power bi.\nPlease see this template.\nLog analytics does have limitations on only allowing you to pull 500k rows at a time.\nI use a amended version of this template and use incremental refresh to keep multiple months of data.\nIf you use a hybrid table you can use direct query to get the most recent data.\nYou will need to also use the fabric capacity app in order to keep track of overall CU usage","date":"2025-03-30"},{"url":"https://blog.crossjoin.co.uk/2024/10/27/new-solution-for-monitoring-power-bi-fabric-gateways/","title":"New Solution For Monitoring Power BI/Fabric Gateways","domain":"blog.crossjoin.co.uk","summary":"A while ago Rui Romano published a gateway monitoring solution that proved to be very popular, but recently my colleague at Microsoft Edgar Cotte built a new open-source solution that builds on Rui’s work and which is even more powerful, using Fabric Real-Time Intelligence to let you analyse gateway log information in real time.\nYou can read his announcement on LinkedIn here and download the solution from https://aka.ms/FabricGatewayMonitoring.\n...\n1. You need Fabric enabled, yes","date":"2024-10-27"},{"url":"https://community.fabric.microsoft.com/t5/Fabric-Ideas/Gateway-performance-monitoring-dashboard/idi-p/4491477","title":"Gateway performance monitoring dashboard","domain":"community.fabric.microsoft.com","summary":"We have a Power BI Fabric capacity metrics app to monitor workspace capacity resource consumption.\nThere, we can filter for capacity name, workspace name, semantic model name, and other details to reach out to the exact issue causing artifacts.\nBut through the Gateway performance monitoring dashboard, we cannot find any of these details; we can only find what kind of data source it is.\nThe consumption details are not useful to investigate the issue creator artifacts, and because of this, we cannot monitor the gateway resource consumption to mitigate the semantic model or data flow resource consumption.\nIf we can have the gateway performance dashboard similarly to the Power BI Fabric Capacity metrics dashboard, it will be very easy to monitor and reduce the performance issues.","date":"2025-02-24"},{"url":"https://community.fabric.microsoft.com/t5/Power-BI-Community-Blog/Power-BI-Gateway-Monitoring-amp-Administrating-Part-1/ba-p/750509","title":"Power BI: Gateway Monitoring & Administrating- Part 1","domain":"community.fabric.microsoft.com","summary":"Now we have the *Service account  * and *Application id * with these we are ready to generate the **Bearer** token and call the API.\n...\nThe below PowerShell script I've used to generate the Bearer token and save it in a Text File\n…\nIn this command you will give the\n1. username\n2. password\n3. clientid\n4. Path where the Bearer token file should be generated\n5. FileName for the Bearer token file\nNow we are all set to create our report in PowerBI\n...\nTricks Here I'm going tell you a trick to use a Special API which you couldn't able to find in the PowerBI REST API Documentation The API is\n```\nhttps://api.powerbi.com/v2.0/myorg/me/gatewayclusters?$expand=memberGateways&$skip=0\n```\nThis API will give you the Main Cluster gateway and it's corresponding clusters gateways\nNow lets continue our M-query editor in the PBI Desktop, before writing the code let's put up the steps needed to achieve our goal\n1. We need to call the Gateway API\n2. In order to call the API we need the Bearer Token which we already have it in a Text File called (Bearer.txt) from the previous powershell script through automation from Task Schedular\nThe Code for this is\n```\nlet\n**TokenType = \"Bearer\",\nToken = Text.FromBinary(File.Contents(\"C:\\Jay\\works\\pbi\\Gateway_Report\\Bearer.txt\")),\nSource = Json.Document(Web.Contents(\"https://api.powerbi.com/v2.0/myorg/me/gatewayclusters?$expand=memberGateways&$skip=0\",[Headers=[Authorization=TokenType & \" \" & Token]])),**\n...\nTricks If you looked into PowerBI Gateways API documentation it is wrongly mentioned that this API will give the status, but actually it will not give you the status of the gateway.\nI've confirmed this from officially from the PowerBI community forum\nInorder to get the current gateway status we need to call another api called Gateways - Get Gateway unforunately this API call we need to for each and every gateways seperately and not as a bulk operation.\nLet's continue our coding, the below code will call the Get Gateway API for each and every gateway id which we obtained from the previous result\n```\n#\"Add Column\" = Table.AddColumn(#\"Expanded options\", \"DataFromURLsColumn\", each Json.Document(Web.Contents(\"https://api.powerbi.com/v1.0/myorg/\",[RelativePath= \"gateways/\" & [id] , Headers=[Authorization=TokenType & \" \" & Token]])))\n```\nNow finally our code looks like the below one\n```\n...\nTips : In order to identify the gateway environment like Production/non-production, you can specify in the **gatewayDepartment ** while configuring the gateway itself, which will be used for us to create filters in our report.","date":"2019-08-07"},{"url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fnJvLvhpp8Q","title":"Fabric, Power BI, and Workspace Monitoring - YouTube","domain":"www.youtube.com","summary":"Learn how to monitor your workspaces, Power BI, and Fabric capacity with the built in features from Microsoft.\nThis session will start from the ground zero and show you how to get started with the built-in governance features of Microsoft Fabric.\nWe will discuss how to use these features effectively and the options available.\nWe will also discuss what to avoid and look out for.\n...\n{ts:1375} of of a month sure exactly okay so luckily Microsoft has has uh in on the workspace level we have in\n{ts:1383} workspace settings we have a new item called monitoring in monitoring uh and and and\n{ts:1391} I stole this actually from uh from Microsoft it's actually just a Microsoft database collecting all the metrics and\n{ts:1399} logs and things that you can listen to inside fabric so it can be capacity consumption it can be notebook\n{ts:1406} executions it could be event house query locks it could be usage of your semantic models all those\n{ts:1413} kind of things that on a daily basis is just being used and those telemetries and those logs are now being made\n{ts:1421} available to us we can't access the raw data but we can access a predefined data set uh given to us and then we can do\n{ts:1431} with that whatever we need to to do um the buildup of this of this workspace monitoring is is actually we get an\n{ts:1439} event stream uh this is the logo for an event stream uh we get an event stream that picks up all the signals coming\n{ts:1446} from Microsoft logging mechanisms and pipes them into an event house into a kql database um this database is also\n{ts:1454} given to us uh and that database contains a lot of objects I'll show you those in just a minute when we dive into\n...\n{ts:1475} it right so you both do powerbi perhaps you have just tried to work with the realtime dashboards and\n{ts:1482} and um but we have we have two options um we can either build our reports ourselves because the data is now\n{ts:1491} available to us it's live because it's from a kql database we don't have to process anything it's just coming to us\n{ts:1497} live uh or we can build a rtime dashboard um or we can and I think was it two\n{ts:1506} weeks ago or perhaps three weeks ago I can't remember but Microsoft released a a a package set of standardized ready to\n{ts:1515} go reports on both power and dashboards to begin to get started with your workspace monitoring um so when you\n{ts:1523} click the menu option in fabric these things are deployed to the event stream and the kql database and you choose and\n{ts:1531} you deploy whatever you need from both powerbi and return desports there's a lot of discussion on\n{ts:1538} when to choose powerbi versus Real Time dashboards um I will say if you need insights within a second when it happens\n...\n{ts:1581} brought both of them um um so um we can we can test them out um a short code here if you want to\n{ts:1591} download them akbi fbon fabric monitoring fabon um then you will get directly to the Microsoft GitHub page to\n{ts:1598} download these two artifacts and then you can get going the\n{ts:1604} official image or drawing or marketing thing that Microsoft does looks like this it's more or less just what I\n...\n{ts:1629} want um there's also perhaps and I'm still being surprised that not many people know this the fabric capacity\n{ts:1638} monitoring app the fabric capacity metric app I think it's called um when I mentioned that to my clients they're\n...\n{ts:1683} fabric capacity metrix app which gives us the tenant level access and view on the capacity and the workspaces and then\n...\n{ts:2205} it's it's just a table so from Microsoft we get these we get these um ready to download items we get a report called\n{ts:2216} fabric work workspace monitor and we get a um a a real time dashboard in adjacent object let me start with the fabric let\n…","date":"2025-02-27"}],"error":null},{"ok":true,"spec":{"query":"Power BI Sentinel gateway monitoring","limit":6},"result":[{"url":"https://www.powerbisentinel.com/whats-new/","title":"What's New? - Power BI Sentinel","domain":"www.powerbisentinel.com","summary":"**As part of our ongoing Fabric integration, For E-1000 licenses and above, we are now providing data for all assets hosted in Fabric capacities, including Fabric Warehouses, Lakehouses, Deployment Pipelines, Dataflows, Notebooks, SQL Endpoints, and more.\n...\n**Enhanced processing of Power BI gateways**\nWe have significantly enhanced and expanded the part of Power BI Sentinel that processes Power BI gateways.\nBy processing each gateway separately, we're now providing a full and up-to-date picture of all data sources currently connected to those gateways, even if those data sources aren't being used by any datasets\n[Released September 2024]","date":"2025-11-27"},{"url":"https://www.powerbisentinel.com","title":"Power BI Sentinel","domain":"www.powerbisentinel.com","summary":"Governance, Auditing and Disaster Recovery for Power BI and Microsoft Fabric\n...\nPower BI Sentinel works alongside your Power BI estate, to provide greater security and governance of your Power BI data.\nBackups and Change Tracking provide disaster recovery.\nAuditing and Usage Analytics gives you visibility over who is doing what, and when.\nLineage Explorer shows you where your sensitive data is being exposed.\n...\nMonitor Power BI usage and track user actions across reports, apps, and workspaces with detailed logging.\n...\nShow which data sources are used by which datasets, data flows, and also by which reports\n...\nPower BI Sentinel delivers enablement, security, and compliance to your Power BI ecosystem.\nIt's designed to keep you in control and minimize your risk whilst enhancing user functionality.","date":"2026-05-08"},{"url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0Enini93Quk","title":"Power BI Dev Camp Session 28 -  How To Monitor Your On-prem Power BI Gateways","domain":"www.youtube.com","summary":"In this Power BI Dev Camp session, Rui demonstrates the pbigtwmonitor solution which he has published to GitHub.\nBy learning how to test and deploy this solution, you can learn how to help Power BI admins quickly access all gateway logs for an entire Power BI tenant and to explore the gateway logs, counters and queries on a pre-built Power BI Report.\n...\n{ts:146} is just a bridge between your on Prem data sources and the power BI tenant.\n...\nIn the complete way, because you can you t use the power BI template.\nI will show you how to do\n{ts:389} it, but if you want to deploy this and if you have a lot t of gateways the main advantage that this solution will l give you it will centralize it\n{ts:399} it will make sure that all the gateway logs they will be.\nUploaded and centralized in the data link and existing data link or the new data link if you want.\n{ts:409} And that, and the way I do it is it's basically having a Powershell script that is deployed on each gateway server.\nSo you need to deploy this script on each gateway server and\n{ts:420} d that script is very simple and lightweight so you can have it running g.\nI won't say every minute, but every 15 minutes every y 30 minutes and the script will save\n{ts:431} e the last execution time and every time it runs it t will get the new logs since the last time it's run n and upload that new log.\n{ts:439} To the Gateway server and the other thing that the script will do is it will apply y some metadata the to the file so it\n{ts:449} t will discover the gateway ID, it will classify each log by year, month and day and partition that in separate folders and it also classified the type of log\n{ts:459} g that are in the gateway logs you have just t verbals and error logs but you also get some report t logs that will get that will log information like.\n{ts:469} Every query that is running on the gateway and also some metadata logs that are very important for example logging.\n...\n{ts:529} easy access to the log, so you don't need as a power BI admin or a tenant t admin.\nYou don't need to give permissions to.\nTo the gateway servers remote desktop permissions\n{ts:539} to the Gateway service to get access to the logs and now you can just share a data Lake connection and an access to a folder or all the folders if you want.\n...\nThe most important things are the\n{ts:749} e scripts.\nSo this is the script that you need to deploy and run on each on each gateway server it\n{ts:756} t has a configuration file.\nThat configuration file the only thing that you should.\nBe required to change is the\n{ts:764} connection string the to the storage account or to the e data lake, everything else.\n…\nYou can do, you just need to download the front desk if you want to the lake and\n{ts:840} have a complete automation of the whole thing.\nYou need to use the from lake, but it's exactly 1 ne more time exactly the same template, so\n{ts:848} that is everything is documented and I'll can you set this up, I will.","date":"2023-02-16"},{"url":"https://www.minceddata.info/2023/10/16/power-bi-gateway-monitoring-and-more-using-microsoft-fabric/","title":"Power BI Gateway monitoring and more using Microsoft Fabric","domain":"www.minceddata.info","summary":"This article describes a solution built with Microsoft Fabric.\nIt’s not necessary to use Fabric, and it’s also not necessary to build a solution on your own.\nIf you want to track the well-being of your on-premises data gateways but do not want to build something, I recommend using the solution by Rui Romano you can find here: https://github.com/RuiRomano/pbigtwmonitor\nI built this monitoring solution focusing on the well-being of the on-premises data gateway.\nI might extend this solution in the future, but for now, it’s about the availability of the on-premises data gateway and the data gateway connections.\n...\nI use Microsoft Fabric because I do not have to care about getting access to many different Azure resources.\nI only need a Microsoft Fabric-enabled workspace and a Service Principal.\nWhile the components of Microsoft Fabric are automatically available when using a Microsoft Fabric-enabled workspace, I have to take care of the Service Principal.\n...\nFor tracking the well-being of the on-premises data gateway(s), I use the REST API endpoint\n`gateways` described here: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/rest/api/power-bi/gateways/get-gateways.\nLater, I will extend this solution to\nother REST APIs from the gateway group.\nI consider monitoring the Power BI environment a good idea, so I consider using the Power BI REST APIs essential.\nBut the REST APIs are not only useful when you want to monitor your Power BI tenant.\n...\nYou can read about the creation of a Service Principal in this article: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/developer/embedded/embed-service-principal?WT.mc_id=DP-MVP-5003068.\nFrom my experience, it’s always a good idea to add the Service Principal to an Azure Active Directory (Azure Entra ID) security group, the reason for this is simple, sometimes (maybe most of the time) it’s not possible to provide permissions to the Service Principal directly inside Power BI.\nInstead, permissions can only be granted via an Azure Entra ID security group.\n...\nFor this reason, you can allow specific REST APIs to be executed by a service principal.\nThis assignment is done in the API permissions blade of the service principal, this is shown in the following visualization:\nThe following image shows how you allow the execution of Power BI REST APIs by the security group the Service Principal is assigned to:\nUsing security groups instead of adding the service principal directly is always a good idea.\nGranting permissions to a service principal should always follow the concept of least privileges.\nFollowing this concept leads to the creation of multiple service principals.\nThis means you need documentation of the service principals you allow access to the APIs of your service - the Power BI Service.\nHowever, documentation of the various service principals is a different topic.\nThe following image shows how you add the Service Principal (not the security group, this is only for demonstration purposes) as an Admin to the on-premises data gateway(s) you want to monitor, the second resource that requires authorization next to the REST API.\n...\nHere, you will find the notebook “tenantMonitoring_gateways.ipynb” (https://tommartens-my.sharepoint.com/:u:/g/personal/tom_minceddata_com/EaxaEdtqMo5FvUdSstAAuoEBmo2PUYj4kXdqEqLCjoFUhA?e=MrF5eU).\n...\nBasically, the current solution is simple: I have a Fabric-enabled workspace, a lakehouse, and a notebook, but the solution will grow during the next iterations.\nThe following image is showing the current state (October 2023) of my architecture:\nInside the lakehouse, I created a subfolder “GatewayInformation,” inside the files section.\nThis is the place where I store the result of a REST API endpoint request as a CSV file, of course, after some data transformations.\nI write the same dataframe (almost the same) that I have written as CSV also as a table in delta format using the vorder sorting.","date":"2023-10-16"},{"url":"https://community.powerbi.com/t5/Power-Query/Monitoring-On-Premises-Data-Gateway/td-p/56371","title":"Monitoring On-Premises Data Gateway - Microsoft Fabric Community","domain":"community.powerbi.com","summary":"I've been using the gateway and other performance monitor counters via PBI Desktop on a project recently.","date":"2019-06-17"},{"url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/guidance/powerbi-implementation-planning-auditing-monitoring-tenant-level-monitoring","title":"Microsoft Sentinel","domain":"learn.microsoft.com","summary":"### Microsoft Sentinel\nMicrosoft Sentinel is a security information and event management (SIEM) service.\nIt's an Azure service that allows you to:\n- Collect logs and security data for users, devices, applications, and infrastructure.\nYou can capture logs and user activities from the Power BI service and many other areas across the enterprise.\n- Detect potential threats.\nYou can create rules and alerts to refine what's important to track.\nAutomated threat intelligence exists as well, which can help reduce the manual effort.\n- Analyze data and investigate the scope and root cause of incidents.\nYou can use built-in visualization tools or Kusto Query Language (KQL) queries.\nYou can also use Power BI to visualize and analyze the data that you collect.\n- Respond to security incidents and threats.\nYou can handle responses directly.\nOr, you can automate responses and remediations by using playbooks, which are workflows based on Azure Logic Apps.\nMicrosoft Sentinel stores its data in Azure Log Analytics (a component of Azure Monitor).\nIt's based on the same architecture as the semantic model event logs, which capture user-generated and system-generated activities that occur for a semantic model.\nYou can use Microsoft Sentinel with Power BI in two different ways.\n- **Use the Power BI data connector in Sentinel**: A subset of the attributes from the Power BI audit logs are streamed into Azure Log Analytics (Azure Monitor).\nIt's one way to obtain audit logs for tracking user activities in your Power BI environment.\nFor more information, see Tenant-level auditing.\n- **Use Power BI as an analytical tool**: Power BI connects to the data that Microsoft Sentinel (and, accordingly, Azure Monitor and Azure Log Analytics) collects from a wide variety of data connectors.\nYou can then use standard Power BI functionality to model, analyze, and visualize data.\nFor more information, see Create a Power BI report from Microsoft Sentinel data.\n...\nWhen there's an apparent service outage or degradation, Power BI administrators and users should refer to the Power BI support site.\nIt's a publicly available site that displays information about high-priority issues concerning Power BI.\nThis site shows:\n- The status for the Power BI service.\n- Service level outages or degradation notifications.\n- Informational messages for broad awareness.\n...\nIf you work with national/regional clouds, work with your Microsoft 365 administrator to monitor Power BI issues.\n...\nYou can receive alert notifications by email to inform you when there's a service outage, interruption, or degradation occurring in your Power BI tenant.\n...\nTo set up email alerts, enable the *Receive email notifications for service outages or incidents* tenant setting.\nBecause its purpose is to send email, you must assign a mail-enabled security group to this setting.\nWe recommend that you use a group name like *Power BI System Support*.\nYou should add your Power BI administrators, key personnel from your Center of Excellence (COE), and your help desk that handles user support to this group.\n...\nPower BI administrators and users can also monitor the Fabric known issues page.\nThis page includes information about currently active known issues and recently closed known issues.\n...\nThe Microsoft 365 admin center displays service health information, incident summaries, and advisory messages for Microsoft 365 services.\n...\nYou can check the publicly available Power BI issues site.\nIt's a place for users to publicly report issues they've encountered.\n...\nWe recommend that you closely monitor releases for the Power BI service and Power BI Desktop.\n...\nConsider routinely monitoring the Power BI ideas site.\n...\n**Checklist** - When planning for tenant-level monitoring, key decisions and actions include:\n- **Educate administrators and key personnel**: Make sure that Power BI administrators and key personnel in the COE are aware of the resources available for monitoring service health, updates, and announcements.\n- **Create a monitoring plan**: Determine how, and who will monitor service health, updates, and announcements.\nEnsure that expectations are clear for how to gather, communicate, plan, and act on the information.\n- **Create a user communication plan**: Clarify which situations warrant communicating to others in the organization.\nDetermine how, and who will be responsible for communicating to users in the organization, and in which circumstances.\n- **Decide who should receive email notifications**: Determine who should receive email notifications from Microsoft when there's a Power BI issue.\nUpdate the *Receive email notifications for service outages or incidents* tenant setting to align with your decision.\n- **Review administrator roles**: Review roles and permissions necessary for viewing service health in the M365 admin center.\n- **Investigate information protection and DLP requirements**: Explore requirements for using sensitivity labels in Microsoft Purview Information Protection to classify data (the first building block of information protection).\nConsider requirements for implementing DLP for Power BI, and the associated monitoring processes.\n- **Investigate Defender for Cloud Apps capabilities**: Explore requirements for using Microsoft Defender for Cloud Apps to monitor user behavior and activities.","date":"2024-12-30"}],"error":null},{"ok":true,"spec":{"query":"devladlog.com Power BI gateway monitoring 2025","limit":6},"result":[{"url":"https://www.devladlog.com/articles/power-bi/2025-07-13-data-gateway-operations-security-and-optimization-playbook-2025/","title":"On-Premises Data Gateway: Operations, Security, and Optimization Playbook (2025)","domain":"www.devladlog.com","summary":"This operations playbook covers the **day-2 operational concerns** for On-Premises Data Gateway — security hardening, monitoring, performance optimization, incident response, and cost management.\n...\n# Security monitoring script for On-Premises Data Gateway\n# Run daily as scheduled task\n$alerts = @()\n# Check 1: Service principal credentials expiring within 30 days\n$apps = Get-MgApplication -All\nforeach ($app in $apps) {\nforeach ($cred in $app.PasswordCredentials) {\n$daysUntilExpiry = ($cred.EndDateTime - (Get-Date)).Days\nif ($daysUntilExpiry -lt 30 -and $daysUntilExpiry -gt 0) {\n$alerts += @{\nType = \"CredentialExpiry\"\nSeverity = if ($daysUntilExpiry -lt 7) { \"Critical\" } else { \"Warning\" }\nMessage = \"$($app.DisplayName) credential expires in $daysUntilExpiry days\"\nAction = \"Rotate credential immediately\"\n}\n}\n}\n}\n# Check 2: Failed sign-in attempts (brute force detection)\n$failedSignIns = Get-MgAuditLogSignIn -Filter \"status/errorCode ne 0\" -Top 100\n$suspiciousIPs = $failedSignIns |\nGroup-Object -Property IpAddress |\nWhere-Object { $_.Count -gt 10 }\nforeach ($ip in $suspiciousIPs) {\n$alerts += @{\nType = \"BruteForce\"\nSeverity = \"Critical\"\nMessage = \"$($ip.Count) failed attempts from $($ip.Name)\"\nAction = \"Block IP in Conditional Access; investigate\"\n}\n}\n# Report\nif ($alerts.Count -gt 0) {\nWrite-Host \"=== SECURITY ALERTS ===\" -ForegroundColor Red\n$alerts | ForEach-Object {\nWrite-Host \"[$($_.Severity)] $($_.Message)\" -ForegroundColor $(\nif ($_.Severity -eq \"Critical\") { \"Red\" } else { \"Yellow\" }\n)\n}\n} else {\nWrite-Host \"No security alerts\" -ForegroundColor Green\n}\n```\n## Monitoring & Observability\n*Figure: Azure Monitor Logs – KQL query results with time-series visualization.*\n### Key Metrics Dashboard\n|Metric|Target|Alert Threshold|Measurement|\n|--|--|--|--|\n|Availability|99.9%|< 99.5%|Synthetic monitoring|\n|API Response Time (P95)|< 2 seconds|> 5 seconds|Application Insights|\n|Error Rate|< 1%|> 5%|Log Analytics|\n|Active Users (DAU)|Trending up|Drop > 20% week-over-week|Usage analytics|\n|Data Sync Latency|< 5 minutes|> 15 minutes|Custom metric|\n|Storage Utilization|< 80%|> 90%|Platform metrics|\n### Alert Configuration\n```\n{\n\"alertRules\": [\n{\n\"name\": \"High Error Rate\",\n\"condition\": \"errorRate > 5% for 5 minutes\",\n\"severity\": \"Critical\",\n\"action\": [\"email-oncall\", \"teams-channel\", \"pagerduty\"],\n\"runbook\": \"https://wiki.internal/runbooks/high-error-rate\"\n},\n{\n\"name\": \"Slow Response Time\",\n\"condition\": \"p95Latency > 5s for 10 minutes\",\n\"severity\": \"Warning\",\n\"action\": [\"teams-channel\"],\n\"runbook\": \"https://wiki.internal/runbooks/slow-response\"\n},\n{\n\"name\": \"Capacity Warning\",\n\"condition\": \"storageUsed > 80%\",\n\"severity\": \"Warning\",\n\"action\": [\"email-admin\"],\n\"runbook\": \"https://wiki.internal/runbooks/capacity-planning\"\n}\n]\n}\n...\n1. **Acknowledge** — Confirm the incident within response time SLA\n2. **Assess** — Determine severity, blast radius, and initial cause\n3. **Communicate** — Notify stakeholders via established channels\n4. **Mitigate** — Apply immediate fix or workaround to restore service\n5. **Resolve** — Implement permanent fix with proper testing\n...\n- Security hardening is not optional — implement the full checklist before production launch\n- Monitor the 6 key metrics continuously; configure alerts with clear runbook links\n- Performance optimization is ongoing — run the analysis script monthly\n- Classify incidents by severity and respond within SLA targets\n- Review costs quarterly; right-size licenses and optimize API call patterns\n- Conduct blameless post-mortems for every SEV-1 and SEV-2 incident","date":"2025-07-13"},{"url":"https://www.devladlog.com/articles/power-bi/2025-11-17-on-premises-data-gateway-configuration-management/","title":"On-Premises Data Gateway: Configuration and Management","domain":"www.devladlog.com","summary":"## Run performance monitoring\nGet-GatewayPerformance -DurationMinutes 5\n```\n...\n☑ Data Source Optimization:\n☐ Add indexes on frequently filtered columns\n☐ Update statistics on large tables\n☐ Implement query folding in Power Query\n...\n### Automated Health Check Script\n```\n## Gateway health monitoring script (run via scheduled task every 5 minutes)\nfunction Test-GatewayHealth {\n```powershell\nparam(\n[string]$EmailTo = \"ops@contoso.com\",\n[string]$SmtpServer = \"smtp.contoso.com\"\n$report = @{\nTimestamp = Get-Date\nServerName = $env:COMPUTERNAME\nChecks = @()\nOverallStatus = \"Healthy\"\n## Check 1: Gateway service running\n$service = Get-Service -Name \"PBIEgwService\" -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue\n$serviceCheck = @{\nName = \"Gateway Service\"\nStatus = if ($service -and $service.Status -eq \"Running\") { \"✅ Pass\" } else { \"❌ Fail\" }\nDetails = \"Status: $($service.Status)\"\n$report.Checks += $serviceCheck\nif ($serviceCheck.Status -like \"*Fail*\") {\n$report.OverallStatus = \"Unhealthy\"\n## Check 2: CPU usage\n$cpu = (Get-Counter \"\\Processor(_Total)\\% Processor Time\").CounterSamples[0].CookedValue\n$cpuCheck = @{\nName = \"CPU Usage\"\nStatus = if ($cpu -lt 80) { \"✅ Pass\" } else { \"⚠️ Warning\" }\nDetails = \"$([math]::Round($cpu, 1))%\"\n$report.Checks += $cpuCheck\nif ($cpu -gt 90) {\n$report.OverallStatus = \"Warning\"\n## Check 3: Available memory\n$memAvailMB = (Get-Counter \"\\Memory\\Available MBytes\").CounterSamples[0].CookedValue\n$memCheck = @{\nName = \"Available Memory\"\nStatus = if ($memAvailMB -gt 2048) { \"✅ Pass\" } else { \"⚠️ Warning\" }\nDetails = \"$([math]::Round($memAvailMB, 0)) MB\"\n$report.Checks += $memCheck\nif ($memAvailMB -lt 1024) {\n$report.OverallStatus = \"Warning\"\n## Check 4: Gateway log errors (last hour)\n$logPath = \"C:\\Users\\*\\AppData\\Local\\Microsoft\\On-premises data gateway\\Gateway*.log\"\n$logs = Get-ChildItem $logPath | Sort-Object LastWriteTime -Descending | Select-Object -First 1\nif ($logs) {\n$recentErrors = Get-Content $logs.FullName -Tail 1000 |\nWhere-Object { $_ -match \"ERROR|FATAL\" } |\nWhere-Object { (Get-Date) - [datetime]::Parse(($_ -split '\\|')[0]) -lt (New-TimeSpan -Hours 1) }\n$errorCheck = @{\nName = \"Recent Errors\"\nStatus = if ($recentErrors.Count -eq 0) { \"✅ Pass\" } else { \"⚠️ Warning\" }\nDetails = \"$($recentErrors.Count) errors in last hour\"\n}\n$report.Checks += $errorCheck\nif ($recentErrors.Count -gt 10) {\n$report.OverallStatus = \"Warning\"\n}\n## Check 5: Disk space\n$disk = Get-WmiObject Win32_LogicalDisk -Filter \"DeviceID='C:'\"\n$freeSpaceGB = [math]::Round($disk.FreeSpace / 1GB, 2)\n$diskCheck = @{\nName = \"Disk Space (C:)\"\nStatus = if ($freeSpaceGB -gt 10) { \"✅ Pass\" } else { \"⚠️ Warning\" }\nDetails = \"$freeSpaceGB GB free\"\n$report.Checks += $diskCheck\nif ($freeSpaceGB -lt 5) {\n$report.OverallStatus = \"Warning\"\n## Generate report\n...\n## Send email if unhealthy or warning\nif ($report.OverallStatus -ne \"Healthy\") {\n$subject = \"⚠️ Gateway Health Alert - $($report.ServerName) - $($report.OverallStatus)\"\nSend-MailMessage -To $EmailTo -From \"gateway-monitor@contoso.com\" -Subject $subject `\n-Body $emailBody -BodyAsHtml -SmtpServer $SmtpServer\nWrite-Host \"Alert sent: $subject\" -ForegroundColor Yellow\n} else {\nWrite-Host \"✅ Gateway health check passed\" -ForegroundColor Green\nreturn $report```\n## Run health check\nTest-GatewayHealth\n```\n...\n## Analyze gateway logs for patterns and issues\nfunction Analyze-GatewayLogs {\n...\n## Run update (with auto-restart)\nUpdate-Gateway -AutoRestart\n...\n☐ Notify users of maintenance window (7 days advance)\n☐ Backup gateway configuration\n☐ Verify cluster health (if applicable)\n☐ Document current version and config\n☐ Prepare rollback plan\n☐ Stage updates on test environment first\n...\n- Keep module, CLI, and SDK versions pinned in automation pipelines and review quarterly.\n...\n- Store secrets in managed secret stores and avoid embedding credentials in scripts or source files.\n- Enable audit logging, data protection policies, and periodic access reviews for regulated workloads.\n...\n- Define budgets and alerts, then monitor usage and cost trends continuously after go-live.\n- Baseline performance with synthetic and real-user checks before and after major changes.\n- Scale resources with measured thresholds and revisit sizing after usage pattern changes.\n...\n- **Automated monitoring** catches issues before they impact users\n- **Security hardening** includes TLS 1.2 enforcement, least privilege, and regular patching\n- **Maintenance windows** should be scheduled monthly with comprehensive pre/post-checks\n- **Troubleshooting** requires systematic approach: logs, connectivity tests, performance metrics\n...\n1. **Assess current gateway deployment**(single node or cluster?)\n2. **Plan high availability implementation**(if not already clustered)\n3. **Configure Kerberos authentication** for Windows auth scenarios\n4. **Implement automated monitoring** with health check scripts\n5. **Establish maintenance schedule** with documented procedures\n6. **Security hardening audit**(TLS, service accounts, access controls)\n7. **Performance baseline** and optimization based on actual workload\n8. **Document disaster recovery** procedures and test quarterly\n9. **Train operations team** on troubleshooting common issues\n10. **Regular capacity planning** reviews (CPU, memory, query load trends)","date":"2025-11-17"},{"url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/data-integration/gateway/service-gateway-performance","title":"Monitor and optimize on-premises data gateway performance","domain":"learn.microsoft.com","summary":"To monitor performance, gateway admins traditionally depended on manually monitoring performance counters through the Windows Performance Monitor tool.\nWe now offer extra query logging and a Gateway Performance PBI template file to visualize the results.\nThis feature provides new insights into gateway usage.\nYou can use it to troubleshoot slow-performing queries.\n...\nThis feature is currently available only for the on-premises data gateway in the standard mode.\nIt's not available for the personal mode.\n...\nThis feature is now turned on by default.\n...\n- **ReportFilePath**: Determines the path where the four log files are stored.\nBy default, this path is either *\\Users\\PBIEgwService\\AppData\\Local\\Microsoft\\On-premises data gateway\\Report* or *\\Windows\\ServiceProfiles\\PBIEgwService\\AppData\\Local\\Microsoft\\On-premises data gateway\\Report*.\nThe path depends on the OS version.\nIf you use a service account for the gateway other than *PBIEgwService*, replace this part of the path with the service account name.\n- **ReportFileCount**: Determines the number of log files of each kind to retain.\nThe default value is 10.\n- **ReportFileSizeInBytes**: Determines the size of the file to maintain.\nThe default value is 104,857,600.\n- **QueryExecutionAggregationTimeInMinutes**: Determines the number of minutes for which the query execution information is aggregated.\nThe default value is 5.\n- **SystemCounterAggregationTimeInMinutes**: Determines the number of minutes for which the system counter is aggregated.\nThe default value is 5.\nAfter you make the changes to the config file, restart the gateway for these config values to take effect.\nThe report files are now being generated in the location that you specified for **ReportFilePath**.\n...\nWhen you turn on this feature, four new log files are created:\n- The Query Execution Report\n- The Query Start Report\n- The Query Execution Aggregation Report\n- The System Counter Aggregation Report\n...\nNow, you can visualize the data that's in the log files.\n1. Download the Gateway Performance PBI template, and open it using Power BI Desktop.\n2. In the dialog that opens, check that the folder path matches the value in **ReportFilePath**.\n3. Select **Load**, and the template file starts loading the data from your log files.\nAll visuals are populated by using the data in the reports.\n4. Optionally, save this file as a PBIX, and publish it to your service for automatic refreshes.\nTo learn more, go to Publish semantic models and reports from Power BI Desktop.\n...\nTo enable extra logging, in the on-premises data gateway app select **Diagnostics** > **Additional logging**.\n...\n1. In the C:\\Program Files\\On-Premises data gateway\\Microsoft.PowerBI.DataMovement.Pipeline.GatewayCore.dll.config file, set the **StreamBeforeRequestCompletes** setting to **True**, and then save.\n```\n...\n<value>True</value>\n</setting>\n```\n2. In **On-premises data gateway** > **Service Settings**, restart the gateway.","date":"2025-06-12"},{"url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0Enini93Quk","title":"How To Monitor Your On-prem Power BI Gateways - YouTube","domain":"www.youtube.com","summary":"In this Power BI Dev Camp session, Rui demonstrates the pbigtwmonitor solution which he has published to GitHub.\nBy learning how to test and deploy this solution, you can learn how to help Power BI admins quickly access all gateway logs for an entire Power BI tenant and to explore the gateway logs, counters and queries on a pre-built Power BI Report.\n...\nIn the complete way, because you can you t use the power BI template.\nI will show you how to do\n{ts:389} it, but if you want to deploy this and if you have a lot t of gateways the main advantage that this solution will l give you it will centralize it\n{ts:399} it will make sure that all the gateway logs they will be.\nUploaded and centralized in the data link and existing data link or the new data link if you want.\n{ts:409} And that, and the way I do it is it's basically having a Powershell script that is deployed on each gateway server.\nSo you need to deploy this script on each gateway server and\n{ts:420} d that script is very simple and lightweight so you can have it running g.\nI won't say every minute, but every 15 minutes every y 30 minutes and the script will save\n{ts:431} e the last execution time and every time it runs it t will get the new logs since the last time it's run n and upload that new log.\n{ts:439} To the Gateway server and the other thing that the script will do is it will apply y some metadata the to the file so it\n{ts:449} t will discover the gateway ID, it will classify each log by year, month and day and partition that in separate folders and it also classified the type of log\n{ts:459} g that are in the gateway logs you have just t verbals and error logs but you also get some report t logs that will get that will log information like.\n{ts:469} Every query that is running on the gateway and also some metadata logs that are very important for example logging.\n...\nThe BLOB storage or the\n{ts:490} data lake will have a folder for each gateway and on each folder you have the logs.\n...\nThe most important things are the\n{ts:749} e scripts.\nSo this is the script that you need to deploy and run on each on each gateway server it\n{ts:756} t has a configuration file.\nThat configuration file the only thing that you should.\nBe required to change is the\n{ts:764} connection string the to the storage account or to the e data lake, everything else.\nYou probably can't leave it by default but if you change the location where the log files\n{ts:776} get stored you we also need to change the location, but by default if you don't do anything it's going to be stored in this location.\nAnd also.\n{ts:787} That's another thing that is very important is these two There are two part of the templates that you can use that is 1 called from disk and one from data\n...\nYou can do, you just need to download the front desk if you want to the lake and\n{ts:840} have a complete automation of the whole thing.\nYou need to use the from lake, but it's exactly 1 ne more time exactly the same template, so\n{ts:848} that is everything is documented and I'll can you set this up, I will.","date":"2023-02-16"},{"url":"https://community.powerbi.com/t5/Power-Query/Monitoring-On-Premises-Data-Gateway/td-p/56371","title":"Monitoring On-Premises Data Gateway - Microsoft Fabric Community","domain":"community.powerbi.com","date":"2019-06-17"},{"url":"https://github.com/RuiRomano/pbigtwmonitor","title":"RuiRomano/pbigtwmonitor - GitHub","domain":"github.com","summary":"This project aim to help organizations with multiple gateway clusters centralize all their gateway logs and reports into a central storage (ADLS Gen 2) and allow easy and quick exploration of those logs either by:\n- Easily access all the gateway logs without having to remote access to the gateway server\n- Explore the logs with a Power BI Report\n- Explore the logs using a SPARK Engine like Azure Synapse Analytics\n...\n- Lake Template - When gateway log data resides in the ADLS Gen 2 lake\n- Disk Template - When gateway log data reside in disk, useful to analyze the logs directly from the gateway server or gateway log export.\nYou dont need to deploy the powershell scripts to use this template, only need to have access to gateway logs.","date":"2025-12-10"}],"error":null}],"total":7,"saved_to":"/home/user/.pplx/search/pplx_sdk_2026-06-30T23-36-15.203712Z_1588e9cf.json"}
